# Belenios 3.0, section 4.15: proof that a ciphertext (alpha, beta)
# encrypts a non-zero value.  www.belenios.org/specification.pdf
#
# The prover publishes A0 = beta^s * y^(-s*r) and proves that A0, in
# base (beta, y), has the same representation as 1 does in base
# (alpha, g).  Writing u = s and v = -s*r, that is the two equations
# below -- and the first of them has the identity as its target.
secrets u v
claims  u v
eq  1    alpha  g
eq  A0   beta   y

# The checker calls this DETERMINED, and that verdict is correct about
# the pattern and wrong about the protocol.  It is the clearest
# deployed instance of the gap the completeness theorem describes.
#
# Read the names as unrelated and the incidence system forces u = 0
# and v = 0, so the relation determines both.  But the names are not
# unrelated: alpha = g^r and beta = y^r g^m.  Eliminating v from the
# first equation leaves A0 = g^(m*u), so u is pinned only when the
# encrypted m is non-zero.  When m is zero the true kernel is a line
# and the relation determines nothing, while every visible pattern
# says otherwise.
#
# What saves the protocol is not the relation.  It is the verifier's
# first step, "check A0 != 1", which is unsatisfiable exactly when
# m = 0.  Belenios closes by hand the gap no checker reading the
# pattern can close, which is what the completeness theorem says must
# happen.

# An instance: one distinct generator per base name, so the
# environment is faithful and the verdict above transfers to it.
# The exponents are ours and carry no meaning; every element of a
# prime-order group is a generator power, so this is simply a way
# to write distinct elements down.
point alpha  2
point g      3
point beta   5
point y      7
